<svelte:head><title>Naming & organization · Review Room</title><meta name="description" content="How show names, episode IDs, scene codes, shots and take numbers fit together." /></svelte:head>
<header class="docs-heading"><p class="docs-kicker">Using Review Room</p><h1>One shot.<br />A history of takes.</h1><p class="docs-lead">Names help people follow the work. Stable IDs keep the same work connected when folders, titles or the chosen take change.</p></header>
<figure class="docs-visual"><div class="docs-code-key"><div><code>BLR</code><span>Blood Rush<br />Show abbreviation</span></div><div><code>EP101</code><span>Season 1, episode 01<br />Episode identifier</span></div><div><code>PRL</code><span>Parking Lot<br />Scene abbreviation</span></div><div><code>0020</code><span>Shot 20<br />Four-digit shot number</span></div><div><code>v003</code><span>Third stored take<br />Assigned by Review Room</span></div></div><figcaption><code>BLR_EP101_PRL_0020_v003</code> identifies a take. The asset code is <code>BLR_EP101_PRL_0020</code>; the version suffix is separate. This is an illustrative example.</figcaption></figure>
<section><h2>Where each part comes from</h2><div class="docs-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Original name or value</th><th>Code</th><th>Derivation and ownership</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Blood Rush</td><td><code>BLR</code></td><td>Creative Studio Pro suggests the show abbreviation. Confirm the code there before creating the production structure.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Season 1, episode 1</td><td><code>EP101</code></td><td><code>EP</code> + season <code>1</code> + episode <code>01</code>. The episode title is separate and does not replace the identifier.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parking Lot</td><td><code>PRL</code></td><td>A suggested scene abbreviation, confirmed in the production plan. A previously chosen code can differ; the API validates its shape, not the spelling of the scene name.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shot number 20</td><td><code>0020</code></td><td>The production shot number, padded to four digits. Steps of ten leave room for inserted shots. It is not the scene’s folder position.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third stored take</td><td><code>v003</code></td><td>Review Room assigns the version number for this asset. A source filename containing <code>v007</code> cannot force version 7.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p>Confirm production codes before integrations use them as identity. A formatter example using <code>PAR</code> is a valid chosen code, but it is not the automatic suggestion for “Parking Lot.”</p></section>
<section><h2>Folders hold assets; assets hold versions</h2><figure class="docs-visual"><pre class="docs-tree">Blood Rush                             project
├── EP101 — The Late Shift              managed episode folder
│   ├── _Deliverables                   episode cuts / teasers
│   └── 010 PRL — Parking Lot           managed scene folder
│       └── BLR_EP101_PRL_0020           asset (shot 20)
│           ├── v001                    stored take
│           ├── v002                    stored take
│           └── v003                    stored take / possible pick
├── _Deliverables                       season-level deliveries
└── _References                         characters, locations, props</pre><figcaption>The tree illustrates Creative Studio Pro organization. Assets and versions are records, not extra physical folders. <code>010</code> orders the scene folder; <code>0020</code> identifies the shot.</figcaption></figure><p>Creative Studio Pro generates and owns its locked folders. Review Room users can add media there, but cannot rename, move or delete the managed structure. Ordinary projects can use any useful folder arrangement and do not need show or shot codes.</p></section>
<section><h2>Keep these four names separate</h2><div class="docs-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Example</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original filename</td><td><code>parking-take.mp4</code></td><td>Preserved upload provenance. It can differ from the displayed production code.</td></tr><tr><td>Asset code</td><td><code>BLR_EP101_PRL_0020</code></td><td>The stable shot identity. Ordinary uploads instead receive codes such as <code>VID_20261024_00001</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Take identifier</td><td><code>BLR_EP101_PRL_0020_v003</code></td><td>The asset code plus its stored take. Inspecting or choosing another version does not rename the asset.</td></tr><tr><td>Blob storage key</td><td>Generated internal object path</td><td>Locates bytes in storage. It is not derived by assembling folder names or production codes. Moving a folder does not move or rename the blob.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section><h2>Dropping a take into a managed folder</h2><p>A filename such as <code>BLR_EP101_PRL_0020_v007.mov</code> can match an existing shot in that locked folder. Review Room adds the next stored version and ignores the filename’s take number. Unmatched files are retained and flagged <strong>Misfiled</strong> so the owner can file them as a take or move them.</p><p>Deliverable codes include <code>BLR_EP101_EPC</code> (episode cut) and <code>BLR_S1_TRL01</code> (season trailer). Reference codes include <code>BLR_REF_CHR_MARA_VOSS</code>. Codes are upper-case and unique within a project; version suffixes are never sent as part of an API asset code.</p></section>
